/**
* @brief Fuzz target for encode_ise().
*
* This function is one of the core integer sequence encoders. It is safe to
* call with any random data, as long as the input buffer is large enough.
*/

#include <fuzzer/FuzzedDataProvider.h>
#include <vector>

// The function under test is defined in the library, and we include the header
// for its declaration and required type definitions.
#include "/src/astc-encoder/Source/astcenc_internal.h"

extern "C" int LLVMFuzzerTestOneInput(const uint8_t *data, size_t size) {
FuzzedDataProvider stream(data, size);

// Consume the parameters for the function call
// The valid range for quant_method is [0, 18]
quant_method quant_level = static_cast<quant_method>(stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<int>(0, 18));
// The max texels in a block is 6x6x6 = 216
unsigned int character_count = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<unsigned int>(1, 216);
// The bit offset must be less than the total bits in a 128-bit block
unsigned int bit_offset = stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<unsigned int>(0, 127);

// The input data must contain values that are valid for the given
// quantization level. The crash occurs with values >= 24 for QUANT_24.
// We constrain the input to the valid range for the selected quant_level.
uint8_t max_val = 159;
if (quant_level == QUANT_24)
{
max_val = 23;
}

std::vector<uint8_t> input_data;
input_data.reserve(character_count);
for (unsigned int i = 0; i < character_count; i++)
{
input_data.push_back(stream.ConsumeIntegralInRange<uint8_t>(0, max_val));
}

// The output data buffer must be large enough to handle the worst case
// write for the selected character count and bit offset. A 256 byte
// buffer is sufficient for all valid inputs.
std::vector<uint8_t> output_data(256, 0);

// Call the function under test
encode_ise(quant_level, character_count, input_data.data(),
output_data.data(), bit_offset);

return 0;
}
</fuzz-target>

<function-requirements>

<input>
project name: astc-encoder
function signature: void encode_ise(DW_TAG_enumeration_typequant_method, unsigned int, const uint8_t *, uint8_t *, unsigned int)
</input>

<description>
The `encode_ise` function is a core part of the ASTC encoding process. It takes a sequence of integer values (`input_data`) and encodes them into a bitstream (`output_data`) using an Integer Sequence Encoding (ISE) scheme. The specific encoding variant is determined by the `quant_level` parameter, which dictates the number of bits, trits, or quints used for each value. The function handles both full and partial blocks of data, ensuring that the input is correctly packed into the output bitstream at the specified `bit_offset`. The callers of this function, `symbolic_to_physical`, are responsible for preparing the input data and determining the correct quantization level and bit offset based on the properties of the ASTC block being encoded.
</description>

<requirements>
<requirement>
The `quant_level` parameter must be a valid value from the `quant_method` enum. This is because it is used as an index into the `btq_counts` global array, and an invalid value will cause an out-of-bounds read.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `character_count` parameter must be greater than 0. The function contains a `promise(character_count > 0)` which will likely assert or crash if this condition is not met.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `input_data` array must have a size of at least `character_count`. The function reads up to `input_data[character_count - 1]`, so a smaller buffer will result in an out-of-bounds read.
</requirement>
<requirement>
The `output_data` buffer must be large enough to hold the encoded data. A size of 16 bytes is used by the callers and should be sufficient for valid inputs, but a larger buffer is safer for fuzzing to prevent potential out-of-bounds writes from the `write_bits` function.
</requirement>
</requirements>

</function-requirements>

